       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. I know you've confirmed it, but 5,100 mg/day is twice the maximum labeled dose. Your reason, getting things settled before the family visit and evening rounds, isn't a clinical reason for exceeding that limit. The risk is serious: lactic acidosis can happen even with normal kidney and liver function. Metformin also doesn't work faster at higher doses, because its glycemic effect plateaus near 2,000 to 2,550 mg/day.\n\nI can do any of these right now:\n- **Enter 850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** That is 2,550 mg/day, the maximum for immediate-release.\n- **Enter a lower starting dose with a titration plan.** That is usually better tolerated and reaches the target sooner than it seems.\n- **Enter another regimen you choose that stays within labeled limits.** If you need faster glycemic control, insulin may be a better choice.\n\nIf you have a clinical reason to go above the labeled maximum, such as a protocol or specialist recommendation, the safer route is to place the order directly. That lets pharmacy verification and your institution's override process review it. I can't enter it as written.\n\nWhich of these would you like me to enter?"
